---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import {path} from '../lib/format';
---
<Base title="Puff Heritage" description="Read the stories and recipes behind Kerala egg puffs and Bengaluru bakery traditions.">
  <div class="shell">
    <header class="page-head"><p class="kicker">Puff Heritage</p><h1>Stories from the bakery counter</h1><p>How a flaky snack made its way into bakery cases, tea breaks, and family recipes.</p></header>
    <div class="heritage-layout">
      <article class="panel heritage-story">
        <p class="kicker">Kochi, Kerala</p>
        <h2>Cochin Bakery’s egg puff story</h2>
        <p>Cochin Bakery traces its start to four brothers from Thalassery who opened a bakery on Cannon Shed Road in 1939. In interviews for Onmanorama’s <em>Served Since</em>, the family recalls introducing puff pastry in the 1960s and using boiled egg as a filling in the early 1970s.</p>
        <p>The family’s recollection is one account of how the egg puff entered Kerala’s bakeries. The documentary also records a different use for the shop counter. The bakery gave writer Vaikom Muhammad Basheer space to sell his books.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="https://www.onmanorama.com/videos/food/kerala-recipes/2026/08/02/who-made-the-first-egg-puff-the-story-of-a-bakery-that-fed-kings-hosted-basheer.html">Watch the bakery interview <Icon name="external" size={16} /></a>
      </article>
      <article class="panel heritage-story">
        <p class="kicker">Bengaluru, Karnataka</p>
        <h2>Inside the Iyengar bakery case</h2>
        <p>In <em>Oota</em>, Meera Vasudev describes how Bengaluru’s Iyengar bakeries adapted baking to local vegetarian tastes. Vegetable puffs sat alongside palya buns, butter biscuits, and eggless cakes. The atlas’s Bengaluru trail follows shops with Iyengar bakery in their names. Menus vary by shop.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="https://rvca.edu.in/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/2-Meera-Vasudev.pdf">Read Meera Vasudev’s <em>Oota</em> (PDF) <Icon name="external" size={16} /></a>
        <a class="text-link" href={path('trails/bengaluru-iyengar-bakery-run/')}>Explore the Bengaluru bakery trail <Icon name="arrow" size={16} /></a>
      </article>
      <article class="panel heritage-story">
        <p class="kicker">From the recipe notebook</p>
        <h2>What goes inside a mutta puff?</h2>
        <p>Puff pastry, spiced onions and a boiled egg give the Kerala bakery-style puff its shape and filling. Maria’s Menu folds half an egg into each pastry with onion masala. Lekshmi’s Kitchen seasons the filling with fennel and serves the puffs hot with tea or coffee.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="https://mariasmenu.com/vegetarian/kerala-egg-puffs">Maria’s Menu bakery-style egg puffs</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://www.lekshmiskitchen.com/recipes/egg-puffs">Lekshmi’s Kitchen egg puffs</a></li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
    <section class="section panel empty-panel"><h2>What’s your bakery story?</h2><p>Tell us about a shop that shaped your neighbourhood, a family recipe, or a puff you grew up eating.</p><a class="button" href={path('contribute/?kind=heritage')}>Share a story</a></section>
  </div>
</Base>
<style>
  .heritage-layout {display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));gap:1rem;}
  .heritage-story {display:grid;gap:.65rem;align-content:start;}
  .heritage-story h2 {font-size:clamp(1.7rem,4vw,2.5rem);}
  .heritage-story ul {padding-inline-start:1.15rem;margin:0;}
  .heritage-story:first-child {grid-row:span 2;background:var(--butter);}
  @media (max-width:43rem) {.heritage-layout {grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);}}
</style>
